# Flow — the two directions content runs in, as wrappers.
#
# `Stack` is one column with real gaps and no card; it is the default container
# for everything, and most screens are a Page around a Stack. `Row` is its
# horizontal twin and WRAPS, always: a row that cannot wrap is a horizontal
# scrollbar waiting for a narrow viewport.
#
# WHY THE GAPS ARE THREE COMPONENTS AND NOT A `gap: text` PARAMETER. `Spacer`
# takes a size name and picks a class with `match`, which is the right shape for a
# leaf. It is not available here: a component has at most one `slot`, and a
# `match` with three arms would need one per arm. So the size is in the name.
# That is the better trade anyway — `StackTight` says what it is at the call site,
# and three names is the whole vocabulary, which is how a design system stays one.
#
#     use Stack():             20px between blocks — the default rhythm
#     use StackTight():         8px — a label and its value, a list of rows
#     use StackLoose():        40px — between sections of a long page
#
# `RowBetween` pushes its two ends apart (a title and its action); `RowEnd` pushes
# everything right (a form's buttons). Both are the same row with one extra class,
# so a row that later needs to wrap or align differently changes in one place.
import "vocabulary.fct"
app FacetLayout:
    component Stack():
        box class "x-l-stack":
            slot
    component StackTight():
        box class "x-l-stack x-l-stack-tight":
            slot
    component StackLoose():
        box class "x-l-stack x-l-stack-loose":
            slot
    component Row():
        row class "x-l-row":
            slot
    component RowBetween():
        row class "x-l-row x-l-row-between":
            slot
    component RowEnd():
        row class "x-l-row x-l-row-end":
            slot
